# Quantum Search by Local Adiabatic Evolution

Jérémie Roland\({}^{1}\) and Nicolas J. Cerf\({}^{1,2}\)

\({}^{1}\)Ecole Polytechnique, CP 165, Université Libre de Bruxelles, 1050 Brussels, Belgium
\({}^{2}\)Jet Propulsion Laboratory, California Institute of Technology, Pasadena, California 91109

*(July 2001)* — arXiv:quant-ph/0107015v1

###### Abstract

The adiabatic theorem has been recently used to design quantum algorithms of a new kind, where the quantum computer evolves slowly enough so that it remains near its instantaneous ground state which tends to the solution. We apply this time-dependent Hamiltonian approach to Grover's problem. We find that, by adjusting the evolution rate of the Hamiltonian so as to keep the evolution adiabatic on each infinitesimal time interval, the total running time is of order \(\sqrt{N}\), matching Grover. This is in contrast with the constant-rate adiabatic approach [1] which gives time of order \(N\).

