## I Introduction

Continuous-time quantum computation with a slowly varying driving Hamiltonian [1,4] is an alternative to gate-based algorithms. Farhi *et al.* [1] applied this to Grover search but obtained only \(T\sim N\). We show that a **locally**-optimal schedule \(s(t)\) restores the quadratic speed-up \(T\sim\sqrt{N}\).

## II Adiabatic theorem

Schrödinger equation

\[i\frac{d}{dt}|\psi(t)\rangle=H(t)|\psi(t)\rangle.\qquad(1)\]

With \(H(t)|E_{k};t\rangle=E_{k}(t)|E_{k};t\rangle\) and minimum gap

\[g_{\min}=\min_{0\leq t\leq T}[E_{1}(t)-E_{0}(t)]\qquad(3)\]

the theorem states \(|\langle E_{0};T|\psi(T)\rangle|^{2}\geq 1-\varepsilon^{2}\) (5) provided

\[\frac{|\langle dH/dt\rangle_{1,0}|}{g_{\min}^{2}}\leq\varepsilon,\qquad\varepsilon\ll 1.\qquad(6)\]

## III Global vs local adiabatic search

Marked-item search over \(N=2^{n}\) items. Uniform initial state

\[|\psi_{0}\rangle=\frac{1}{\sqrt{N}}\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}|i\rangle,\qquad(7)\]

driving Hamiltonians

\[H_{0}=I-|\psi_{0}\rangle\langle\psi_{0}|,\qquad H_{m}=I-|m\rangle\langle m|,\qquad(8,9)\]

interpolation

\[\tilde{H}(s)=(1-s)H_{0}+sH_{m},\quad s=t/T.\qquad(11)\]

Gap in the 2-D invariant subspace:

\[g(s)=\sqrt{1-4\frac{N-1}{N}s(1-s)}.\qquad(13)\]

\(g_{\min}=1/\sqrt{N}\) at \(s=1/2\); \(|\langle d\tilde{H}/ds\rangle_{1,0}|\leq 1\) (14).

**Linear schedule** (Farhi *et al.* [1]): globally-adiabatic requires

\[T\geq N/\varepsilon\qquad(15)\]

— no quantum speed-up.

**Local-adiabatic schedule** (this paper): saturate the adiabatic condition locally,

\[\frac{ds}{dt}=\varepsilon g^{2}(s)=\varepsilon\left(1-4\frac{N-1}{N}s(1-s)\right),\qquad(17)\]

which integrates to

\[t=\frac{N}{2\varepsilon\sqrt{N-1}}\Big[\arctan\!\big(\sqrt{N-1}(2s-1)\big)+\arctan\!\big(\sqrt{N-1}\big)\Big].\qquad(18)\]

Setting \(s=1\) and taking \(N\gg 1\),

\[\boxed{\;T=\frac{\pi}{2\varepsilon}\sqrt{N}\;}\qquad(19)\]

— Grover-scaling recovered in the adiabatic paradigm.

## IV Optimality (Appendix)

Decomposing \(\tilde{H}(s)=\tilde{H}_{1}(s)+\tilde{H}_{2m}(s)\) with \(\tilde{H}_{2m}=-s|m\rangle\langle m|\), and following the Farhi–Gutmann style bound, one obtains

\[T\geq\frac{\varepsilon\,N-1}{4\,\sqrt{N}}\;\Rightarrow\;T\geq\frac{\varepsilon}{4}\sqrt{N}\quad(N\gg 1),\qquad(38,39)\]

so \(T=\Theta(\sqrt{N})\) is asymptotically optimal for this Hamiltonian family.

## V Conclusion

Local-adiabatic scheduling gives \(T=(\pi/2\varepsilon)\sqrt{N}\), the adiabatic-computation analog of Grover. The trick relies on \(g(s)\) being known analytically and independent of the unknown solution — a condition that does not hold for NP-complete Hamiltonians in general.

